### Research Scout note — 2026-09-12: SQLite session-store failure pattern worth importing into scheduler design

Fresh primary-source evidence: NousResearch Hermes Agent v0.21.2 (released 2026-09-11) is a dedicated `state.db` reliability patch after a connection-handling rewrite caused real multi-writer/WAL failures: second writers cancelled POSIX locks; dashboards opened writable handles unnecessarily; checkpoint/repair could run under live holders; healthy WAL stores were misclassified as corrupt; one malformed row could kill whole listings; profile races could bind/read another profile's DB. Release: https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/releases/tag/v2026.9.11

Decision-relevant implication for `LOCAL-MARKET-SCHEDULER`: if SQLite is used as the fast event/lease journal, treat it as a deliberately narrow state boundary rather than a casually shared file. Cheap invariants to preserve/test:

- one explicit writer ownership path; readers open read-only unless mutation is required;
- tracked connection registry rather than ad-hoc `open()` on a live DB;
- no checkpoint/VACUUM/repair unless lock ownership/safety is proven;
- isolate unrelated profile/tenant state instead of multiplexing everything into one writable DB;
- malformed optional/index rows degrade locally rather than poisoning canonical lease/task state;
- recovery must distinguish WAL/index damage from whole-store corruption;
- task/session lookup must remain namespaced by principal/project, never scan sibling stores by bare ID.

This is **not** a new execution request and does not change #126 authority. It is a compact upstream regression corpus/design warning for any successor implementing the local SQLite/event journal. The evidence strengthens the existing preference that Git/Forgejo remains durable canonical authority while SQLite is replaceable fast state/cache. Luna/Light quota policy unchanged; no Codex allocation requested.
